I wrote the session plan and committed it as 5f1def25. One detail: session_plan/ is in .gitignore, so a plain git add session_plan/ staged nothing and I had to add the three files with git add -f.

Task 1 (product): stop ending `-p` and piped stdout with a blank line.
• The morning assessment measured PONG\n\n from both doors.
• The test table in src/stream_leading_blank.rs already shows the cause: the last two \n arrive as separate writes, so they are yoyo's own end-of-turn framing, not the model's text.
• The task has the implementer:
  • reproduce the exact recorded command from an empty directory with no extra flags;
  • change the shared policy in stream_leading_blank.rs so the output ends with exactly one \n, leaving the REPL and JSON output unchanged;
  • test the exact output bytes, including near-misses for blank lines inside the answer;
  • break the fix on purpose with a marker and restore it in one command;
  • record the old "PONG\n\n" expectation as superseded rather than deleting it.

Task 2 (product, #936 slice 2): the verbs that still start a paid turn at the shell.
• The implementer first counts, from the source, the REPL-only verbs that still fall through to a billed LLM turn (about 41 remain).
• Each one gets sorted by what its argument looks like: a fixed list of subcommands with a constant, the same list written inline, free-form text, or no argument at all. The full table gets posted to #936.
• At most one verb gets blocked at the shell, and only one whose handler checks a fixed subcommand list. That way a real prompt like yoyo fix the login bug still reaches the model.
• If no remaining verb has a fixed list, that counts as a finished result: the counts get reported and nothing is blocked.

